      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine how many positive factors of 96 are also multiples of 12, we start by finding the prime factorization of 96 and 12.\n\nFirst, the prime factorization of 96 is:\n\\[ 96 = 2^5 \\times 3 \\]\n\nNext, the prime factorization of 12 is:\n\\[ 12 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\]\n\nA number is a multiple of 12 if it has at least \\(2^2\\) and \\(3\\) in its prime factorization. Therefore, we need to find the factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12, which means they must include at least \\(2^2\\) and \\(3\\) in their factorization. So, the factor must be of the form \\(2^a \\times 3^b \\times \\text{(other primes)}\\) where \\(2 \\leq a \\leq 5\\) and \\(0 \\leq b \\leq 1\\).\n\nSince the factor must be a multiple of 12, we fix \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 1\\) for simplicity. The remaining primes (if any) can be 1 or more, but we don't need to consider them because they will still be in the form \\(2^a \\times 3^b\\) with the minimum factor of 2 included.\n\nNow we calculate the number of possible values for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n- \\(a\\) can be 2, 3, 4, or 5 (4 choices).\n- \\(b\\) can be 0 or 1 (2 choices).\n\nThe total number of factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12 is the product of the number of choices for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[ 4 \\times 2 = 8 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of positive factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{8} \\]",
